# GPG and encryption

The user's GPG key is on a YubiKey that stays connected, and its PIN is usually cached.

- Run GPG signing, decryption, and encryption yourself, including SOPS re-encryption and edits; when a pinentry dialog appears, let it reach the user.
- Stop and report only when the PIN is rejected, the prompt times out, or the card is missing.
- Never print decrypted values, pass them as arguments, or open decrypted files with the file tools; pipe decrypted output straight into the command that consumes it.
- Leave card administration and key management to the user.
- For a new SOPS setup or secret file, use the card key `{{ .user.gpgKey }}` as the user's recipient, plus a machine's own recipient when it must decrypt without the card. Keep the existing method in a project that already has one.

# File I/O

- Use the harness's native read, write, edit, or patch tools for routine file reads, writes, and edits when a suitable tool is available.
- Use shell commands or scripts such as Python and `sed` for file I/O only when the harness has no suitable native tool or the operation requires a command-line tool.
- The GPG and encryption rules take precedence for decrypted output: pipe it straight into the consuming command without opening decrypted files with file tools.

# Git

- Before the first push, rename a branch whose name does not describe the change, such as a worktree codename or random hex, with `git branch -m`. Follow the project's documented naming rule; otherwise name it `<kind>/short-kebab-slug` with a full-word GitHub Flow kind: `feature/`, `fix/`, `docs/`, `chore/`, or `refactor/`. Never rename onto or push over an existing branch.
- When a commit hook fails because mise does not trust a `mise.toml` in a temporary copy the hook made, retry that commit once with `MISE_TRUSTED_CONFIG_PATHS="$(cd "${TMPDIR:-/tmp}" && pwd -P)"`. Do not run `mise trust` on the copy or skip the hook with `--no-verify`.
- Before each push, find the learnings under the project's Compound Engineering `solutions/` directory that cite a path or name the pushed commits delete, move, or rename. Refresh each with `ce-compound-refresh`, commit each refresh separately on the current branch, then push once.

# Pull and merge requests

- Treat GitLab like GitHub: when a Compound Engineering skill speaks only GitHub, do the same steps with `glab`, and skip only a step with no GitLab counterpart, naming it in your report.
- When you run `gh`, `glab`, or another CLI that reads the host and repository from the current Git checkout in a directory whose remotes do not name the target repository, such as a temporary directory or a clone whose `origin` is a local path, pass the host and repository explicitly. Read them from the target project's own checkout, from its `upstream` remote when that points at a server and otherwise from `origin`, never from a default such as `github.com` or `gitlab.com`. Pass `--repo HOST/OWNER/REPO` to a `gh` subcommand that takes `--repo`, and pass `HOST/OWNER/REPO` as the argument of one that takes the repository as an argument, such as `gh repo view`. Give `glab` the repository's full URL instead, through `--repo` or as the argument, because it can read a bare `HOST/OWNER/REPO` as a project on `gitlab.com`. Prefix any other `gh` command, `gh api` included, with `GH_REPO=HOST/OWNER/REPO` and any other `glab` command, `glab api` included, with `GITLAB_REPO` set to the full URL, for that one command and never exported, and also pass `--hostname HOST` to `gh api`, which ignores the host in `GH_REPO`. In the same way, prefix every `glab` command with `GITLAB_HOST=HOST`, because `glab repo clone` and `glab repo delete` send their requests to `gitlab.com` without it, even with a full URL or `GITLAB_REPO`. Write a branch into an API endpoint yourself, because `{branch}` and `:branch` still come from the current checkout.
- After you open or push to a pull or merge request, babysit it until it looks ready to merge, then leave the merge to the user. Babysit a draft only when asked. On GitLab, call it ready only when `head_pipeline` passed on the current `sha`, `detailed_merge_status` is `mergeable`, and no discussion is unresolved.
- Attach the screenshots or recordings a reader needs to see the change. Before uploading, cover secrets, personal data, and internal hosts with opaque boxes, never blur, and strip metadata with `ffmpeg -map_metadata -1`. Upload through `gh --attach` or the GitLab uploads API, never a browser.
- When you bring a pushed pull or merge request branch you opened, or whose rebase the user explicitly authorized, up to date with its base, fetch the base, rebase the branch onto it, and push with `git push --force-with-lease=<branch>:<sha>`. Never merge the base into the branch, including through a skill's or forge tool's merge-based update such as GitHub's update-branch endpoint.
- Before that rebase, record the request's current head SHA as the forge reports it, and use it as `<sha>` in the lease. Confirm with `git merge-base --is-ancestor <sha> HEAD` that the forge's head is an ancestor of your local branch tip and that your checkout has no uncommitted changes. If either check fails, neither rebase nor reset; report the branch. Rebasing a tip that lacks forge-only commits would pass the lease and delete them.
- Run that rebase locally with `git rebase`, never with `gh pr update-branch`, GitHub's update-branch endpoint, or `glab mr rebase`, because those rewrite commits on the server and drop your GPG signatures. When signing or a hook fails during the rebase, run `git rebase --abort` and report, and never disable signing or pass `--no-verify`.
- When the commits since the base include a merge commit, compare the rebased tip's tree with the tree `git merge-tree --write-tree <base> <old-tip>` prints for the base and your pre-rebase tip before you push, and when it reports a conflict or the trees differ, return the branch to that tip with `git reset --hard <old-tip>`, push nothing, and report the branch, because a default rebase drops a change made only inside a merge commit.
- When that rebase conflicts, resolve only conflicts confined to the request's own changes whose resolution you can verify, and otherwise run `git rebase --abort`, push nothing, and report the branch as a blocker for the user.
- When the lease is rejected, never retry with `--force`; report the branch instead.
- Force-push only the head branch of a pull or merge request you opened, in this or an earlier session, or whose rebase the user explicitly authorized, only with `--force-with-lease`, and never a base branch. For any other request behind its base, report the branch as a blocker instead of rebasing or merging.
- On GitHub, `ce-babysit-pr` updates a behind branch through the host and repairs a conflicting one with a local merge; in place of both, keep its `branch_currency` trigger and its claim, outcome, and confirm bookkeeping, and run the rebase above, gated on your proven direct-push authority to the exact head ref rather than on `host_branch_update_capability`.
- On GitLab, run the rebase above when `glab mr view` reports `detailed_merge_status` as `need_rebase` or `conflict`, use the merge request's `sha` as `<sha>` in the lease, and report the outcome in your babysitting summary.

# Markdown

- When you write a range in text that renders as Markdown, in any language, write it in prose, not in a code span, with a dash, as in `1-3` or `10:00-12:00`, or an escaped tilde, as in `1\~3`, and never with a bare `~` or `~~`, so tildes never render as strikethrough.
- Treat a tilde that marks an approximation, such as `~5 min`, the same way: write "about 5 min" or `\~5 min` in prose, not in a code span.
- This covers chat replies, pull request and issue bodies and comments, and Markdown documents; put any other tilde, such as a path like `~/.config` or a Git revision like `HEAD~1`, in a code span, and leave a tilde inside a code span or code block as it is.
- In text that does not render as Markdown, such as commit messages, code comments, and plain-text or config files, write a range with a dash or a word, and an approximation with a word, and never with `\~`.

# Korean text

- Korean text that outlasts the conversation is covered text: commit messages, pull and merge request bodies and comments, issue bodies and comments, documents, Korean inside code such as comments, string literals, user-facing messages, logs, and localization resources, and a closing report, summary, or handoff to the user.
- A short conversational chat reply is not covered text.
- Before you write covered text, load the `humanizer`, `grammar-checker`, and `style-guide` skills, once per session.
- Write covered text by those skills' rules, check it with all three before you publish it, and apply every correction you find.
- Apply them silently: do not paste their analysis tables, grades, or change summaries into the text or the conversation.
- These instructions outrank those skills' own procedures: publish only the corrected text, never call `AskUserQuestion` or stop for confirmation on their behalf, and infer the closest document type when it is unclear.
- Where those skills' rules conflict with the Markdown section above, such as a bare tilde in a range, follow the Markdown section.
- Leave text the user asks you to post as written, and verbatim quotes, unchanged.
- When a report also goes through `ce-noslop`, run `ce-noslop` first, then `humanizer`, `grammar-checker`, and `style-guide` in that order.
- When `ce-code-review`, `ce-doc-review`, or `ce-simplify-code` meets Korean text in the diff or document under review, audit that text with all three skills and report each problem as a finding of that review, whoever wrote the text.

# Tests

- Load the `test-audit` skill before writing, changing, reviewing, or deleting a test.
- When `ce-code-review` or `ce-simplify-code` meets a changed test, audit it with `test-audit` and report each problem as a finding of that review.
- Use the skill's `CAMPAIGN.md` only when the user asks to prune a whole subsystem's tests.

# Review findings

- Assess every finding that a review, verification, or check step of any Compound Engineering skill reports about the work the workflow produces or changes, including testing gaps and residual risks.
- A verdict that chooses among alternatives, such as an adoption grade or candidate verdict, is not a finding.
- Apply substantiated defects and requirement violations in the same change, regardless of severity, confidence labels, or which list contains them. A skill's recommendation to defer or dismiss a finding does not replace your assessment.
- Judge each finding against the actual code, requirements, supported behavior, and concrete failure path or improvement. Consider impact, likelihood, and implementation and maintenance cost.
- Reject a finding when evidence shows its facts are wrong, it requires assumptions outside supported behavior, or it depends on a speculative failure chain without a credible execution path.
- Reject preference-only suggestions with no basis in project rules, clarity, or maintainability, and refactoring suggestions with no concrete benefit.
- Reject added complexity for a vanishingly unlikely edge case only when the expected harm does not justify the implementation and maintenance cost; record the failure scenario, likelihood evidence, impact, and cost.
- Rarity alone does not justify rejecting a credible security or data-loss defect, and a reproducer is not required when code analysis establishes the failure path. Investigate uncertain claims before deciding; lack of evidence is not proof that a defect is impossible.
- Do not dismiss a substantiated problem merely because it is outside the diff, low impact, or costly to fix. Choose the least costly adequate fix, including changes outside the diff when needed, and explain material trade-offs.
- Assess findings yourself without asking for routine approval to reject them, and record each rejection with its evidence or concrete reason.
- Leave a substantiated finding unapplied when every fix would weaken a safety check, the user declined its fix, its fix awaits a user decision, or no change in this repository can fix it.
- List unapplied findings under an `Unapplied review findings` heading in the pull request body, or in the final report when there is no pull request. Start each entry with one of these tags and its evidence or reason: `verified wrong`, `unsupported scenario`, `preference only`, `no concrete benefit`, `disproportionate complexity`, `weakens a safety check`, `declined by you`, `awaits your decision`, or `no fix in this repository`. Omit the section when there are none.
- A fix that would reverse a decision the user made, a brainstorm Scope Boundary or `session-settled` entry included, is asked in every run with a person present, autonomous runs included, and listed as `awaits your decision` in a run with no person present.
- When any other fix needs a user decision, such as a product or scope change, ask in an interactive run; in an autonomous run, apply the recommended fix and report it.
- When the user asked only for a review, report the assessed findings and ask once whether to apply all substantiated fixes.
- Report what you applied and rejected in the pull request body and the final report.
